      "listening_script": "Tutor: So, tell me how the research proposal is coming along. Marcus: We've settled on our topic — urban green spaces and mental wellbeing. Priya: We think it's a good fit because there's plenty of existing literature to build on. Tutor: Good choice. What's your research question at this point? Marcus: We're asking whether proximity to a park affects self-reported stress levels in city residents. Tutor: That's specific, I like that. How are you planning to gather data? Priya: We were thinking of a survey, maybe two hundred responses, distributed online. Tutor: Two hundred is ambitious for a project this size — have you considered a smaller, more targeted sample? Marcus: That's fair, we could focus on one suburb rather than the whole city. Priya: Yes, and that would let us also do a few short interviews for more depth. Tutor: Exactly, mixing methods like that will strengthen your findings. What about your timeline? Marcus: We're aiming to finish the survey design by next Friday. Priya: And run the interviews the following week, so we can start analysis by the third week of the month. Tutor: That sounds workable, just make sure you pilot the survey with a few people first, so you can fix any confusing questions before it goes out properly. Marcus: Good idea, we hadn't planned for that. Tutor: One last thing — remember your ethics application needs to be submitted before you collect any data at all, so get that in as soon as possible. Priya: We'll submit it this week.",
