    {
      "type": "listening",
      "section_number": 4,
      "title": "Lecture: Coral Reef Ecosystems",
      "instructions": "You will hear part of a lecture on coral reef ecosystems. Answer questions 31-40.",
      "listening_script": "Today I want to introduce you to one of the most productive ecosystems on the planet: coral reefs. Despite covering less than one percent of the ocean floor, reefs support around a quarter of all known marine species, which makes them disproportionately important for biodiversity. Corals themselves are not plants, as many people assume, but colonies of tiny animals called polyps. Each polyp secretes a hard calcium carbonate skeleton, and over time these skeletons build up to form the reef structure we recognise. Most reef-building corals rely on a partnership with microscopic algae called zooxanthellae, which live inside the coral's tissue. The algae photosynthesise and share the resulting nutrients with the coral, while the coral provides the algae with shelter and compounds it needs to photosynthesise. This relationship is mutually beneficial, but it's also fragile. When water temperatures rise even slightly above normal, corals become stressed and expel their zooxanthellae, a process known as coral bleaching. Without the algae, the coral loses both its main food source and its colour, turning pale white. If temperatures return to normal quickly, corals can sometimes recover, but prolonged heat stress often leads to coral death. Beyond temperature, reefs also face threats from ocean acidification, which makes it harder for polyps to build their skeletons in the first place, and from sediment runoff caused by coastal development, which can smother coral colonies entirely. Conservation efforts today focus on several approaches: establishing marine protected areas where fishing and development are restricted, breeding heat-resistant coral strains in laboratories, and, more recently, transplanting nursery-grown coral fragments directly onto damaged reefs. None of these approaches alone will reverse global reef decline, but together they may buy reefs enough time to adapt.",
      "questions": [
