    {
      "type": "reading",
      "section_number": 1,
      "title": "The Rise of Urban Beekeeping",
      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer questions 1-13. For questions 1-6, choose True, False, or Not Given.",
      "passage_text": "Over the past two decades, beekeeping has moved from a predominantly rural pursuit to an increasingly common feature of city life. Rooftops in London, New York, and Melbourne now host thousands of hives, and municipal governments in several countries have actively encouraged the trend through subsidies and simplified permitting. The reasons behind this shift are varied, but two stand out: growing public awareness of pollinator decline, and a broader cultural interest in local, small-scale food production.\n\nHoneybee populations have faced well-documented pressures over recent decades, including habitat loss, pesticide exposure, and the spread of parasitic mites such as Varroa destructor. Media coverage of these declines, often framed around the catch-all term 'colony collapse disorder,' brought pollinator health into mainstream public consciousness in a way that had rarely happened before. For many urban residents, keeping a hive became a tangible way to respond to an environmental problem that otherwise felt abstract and distant.\n\nCity environments, somewhat counterintuitively, can offer real advantages for bee colonies. Urban areas typically host a wider variety of flowering plants across a smaller area than agricultural land, where a single crop may dominate the landscape for miles and flower for only a few weeks each year. Parks, gardens, and even roadside verges provide a near-continuous supply of nectar and pollen throughout the growing season. Urban bees are also generally exposed to fewer agricultural pesticides than their rural counterparts, since large-scale crop spraying is far less common within city limits.\n\nHowever, urban beekeeping is not without its critics. Some ecologists argue that the rapid proliferation of managed honeybee hives in cities may be doing more harm than good for pollinator diversity as a whole. Honeybees are a single species, and where hive density becomes very high, they can outcompete wild native bee species for the same limited floral resources. Native bees, many of which are solitary and far less visible than honeybees, often perform pollination services that honeybees cannot replicate as effectively for certain native plants. A city that celebrates 'save the bees' campaigns centred entirely on honeybee hives, these critics suggest, may be missing the more pressing conservation need: protecting habitat for wild pollinators.\n\nThis tension has prompted some cities to reconsider their approach. Rather than simply permitting more hives, several municipal governments have begun pairing beekeeping initiatives with wildflower planting programs designed to expand the overall floral resource base, so that the introduction of managed honeybee colonies does not come at native bees' expense. Advocates of this dual approach argue that healthy pollinator ecosystems, urban or otherwise, depend on diversity rather than the success of any single species.\n\nFor the individual urban beekeeper, the appeal often has as much to do with food and community as ecology. Rooftop honey has become something of a novelty product in some cities, sold at a premium in farmers' markets and specialty grocers, its flavour shaped by the particular mix of local flora. Community beekeeping associations have also sprung up in many cities, offering training courses, shared equipment, and mentorship for newcomers who may otherwise find the practical requirements of hive management daunting. Whether the movement ultimately proves beneficial for pollinators overall may depend less on the number of hives installed than on how thoughtfully cities balance honeybee-friendly policy with support for the wider pollinator community.",
      "questions": [
        { "id": "r1q1", "question_text": "Municipal governments have discouraged urban beekeeping through strict regulation.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "False", "explanation": "The passage says governments encouraged the trend through subsidies and simplified permitting." },
        { "id": "r1q2", "question_text": "Colony collapse disorder is mentioned as a term that raised public awareness of bee decline.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "True", "explanation": "The passage states this term brought pollinator health into mainstream consciousness." },
        { "id": "r1q3", "question_text": "Agricultural land generally offers a greater variety of flowering plants than cities.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "False", "explanation": "The passage says urban areas host a wider variety of flowering plants than agricultural land, where a single crop often dominates." },
        { "id": "r1q4", "question_text": "Urban bees are exposed to more pesticides than rural bees.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "False", "explanation": "The passage states urban bees are generally exposed to fewer agricultural pesticides." },
        { "id": "r1q5", "question_text": "All ecologists support the continued expansion of urban honeybee keeping.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "False", "explanation": "The passage describes ecologists who criticise high hive density for outcompeting native bees." },
        { "id": "r1q6", "question_text": "Rooftop honey is more expensive than average because of a government tax.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "Not Given", "explanation": "The passage mentions premium pricing but never attributes it to any tax." },
        { "id": "r1q7", "question_text": "What parasite is named as a pressure on honeybee populations?", "type": "fill_blank", "correct_answer": "Varroa destructor", "explanation": "The passage names this mite directly." },
        { "id": "r1q8", "question_text": "Why can honeybees outcompete native bees in cities, according to critics?", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["High hive density competing for limited floral resources", "Honeybees are more resistant to pesticides", "Native bees cannot survive in cities at all"], "correct_answer": "High hive density competing for limited floral resources", "explanation": "The passage says very high hive density can lead honeybees to outcompete native bees for the same resources." },
        { "id": "r1q9", "question_text": "What kind of bee species is described as often solitary and less visible?", "type": "fill_blank", "correct_answer": "native bees", "explanation": "The passage describes native bees this way." },
        { "id": "r1q10", "question_text": "What have some cities begun pairing beekeeping initiatives with?", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Wildflower planting programs", "Pesticide bans", "Honey tax incentives"], "correct_answer": "Wildflower planting programs", "explanation": "The passage says cities have paired beekeeping with wildflower planting to expand floral resources." },
        { "id": "r1q11", "question_text": "According to the passage, what do healthy pollinator ecosystems depend on?", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Diversity rather than one species' success", "Maximum honeybee hive numbers", "Elimination of native bee competition"], "correct_answer": "Diversity rather than one species' success", "explanation": "Advocates argue healthy pollinator ecosystems depend on diversity rather than any single species." },
        { "id": "r1q12", "question_text": "What do community beekeeping associations offer newcomers?", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["Training, shared equipment, and mentorship", "Free land to build hives", "Government grants only"], "correct_answer": "Training, shared equipment, and mentorship", "explanation": "The passage lists these as the offerings of community associations." },
        { "id": "r1q13", "question_text": "What ultimately may matter more than the number of hives installed?", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["How thoughtfully policy balances honeybees and wider pollinators", "The price of rooftop honey", "The height of the rooftops used"], "correct_answer": "How thoughtfully policy balances honeybees and wider pollinators", "explanation": "The final sentence states this balance may matter more than hive numbers." }
      ]
