    {
      "type": "reading",
      "section_number": 2,
      "title": "Remote Work and Organisational Culture",
      "instructions": "Read the passage and answer questions 14-26. For questions 14-19, choose True, False, or Not Given.",
      "passage_text": "The shift toward remote and hybrid work arrangements, accelerated sharply by the events of 2020, has settled into something more durable than early commentators predicted. What began as an emergency measure has, in many industries, become a permanent feature of how organisations operate, prompting a broader reassessment of assumptions that had gone largely unchallenged for decades: that productive work requires physical co-location, and that organisational culture can only be built and maintained face to face.\n\nEarly research on remote work productivity produced mixed and sometimes contradictory findings, in part because studies conducted during the initial period captured a workforce operating under exceptional circumstances rather than under conditions representative of long-term remote arrangements. Employees were frequently managing childcare, health anxieties, and makeshift home offices simultaneously, factors that complicate any clean comparison with pre-pandemic office productivity. More recent studies, conducted once remote arrangements had stabilised into routine, generally find that individual task-based productivity is comparable to, and in some cases higher than, office-based work, particularly for roles involving focused, independent tasks.\n\nWhere the picture becomes more complicated is in the domain of collaborative and creative work. Several studies have found that spontaneous, informal interactions — the so-called 'water cooler conversations' — decline substantially in fully remote settings, and that this decline correlates with reduced cross-team idea generation over time. Formal virtual meetings, researchers note, tend to replace the structured components of collaboration reasonably well, but struggle to replicate the unplanned, low-stakes exchanges that often produce unexpected insights or connections between otherwise unrelated projects.\n\nThis has led many organisations toward hybrid models, which attempt to preserve the productivity benefits of remote work for focused tasks while reintroducing in-person time for activities that appear to benefit most from physical presence. The specific ratio of in-office to remote time varies considerably between organisations, and there is, as yet, no strong consensus on an optimal split; some companies have settled on two or three fixed in-office days per week, while others allow individual teams to determine their own arrangements based on the nature of their work.\n\nOrganisational culture presents a related but distinct challenge. Culture, in the sociological sense used in workplace research, refers to the shared norms, values, and unwritten rules that shape how people behave and make decisions within an organisation. Critics of fully remote work have long argued that culture is transmitted primarily through observation and informal socialisation — watching how colleagues handle disagreements, noticing what behaviours get rewarded or ignored — processes that are difficult to replicate over video calls. Proponents of remote work counter that culture can be, and often has been, deliberately documented and taught rather than left to informal osmosis, pointing to remote-first organisations that have built strong, cohesive cultures through explicit written norms, structured onboarding, and deliberate communication practices.\n\nWhat seems increasingly clear is that neither extreme position — a wholesale return to mandatory office attendance, nor a fully remote model applied uniformly across all roles — adequately reflects the variation in how different types of work benefit from physical proximity. The organisations navigating this transition most successfully appear to be those treating the question empirically, adjusting policy based on observed outcomes for their specific teams rather than applying a single rule across an entire organisation.",
      "questions": [
        { "id": "r2q14", "question_text": "Remote work arrangements have mostly reverted to pre-2020 norms.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "False", "explanation": "The passage says remote/hybrid work has become a permanent, durable feature rather than reverting." },
        { "id": "r2q15", "question_text": "Early studies on remote work productivity were conducted under typical, stable conditions.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "False", "explanation": "The passage says early studies captured exceptional circumstances, not representative long-term conditions." },
        { "id": "r2q16", "question_text": "More recent studies find individual task-based productivity remote is generally comparable to or better than office work.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "True", "explanation": "The passage states this directly for focused, independent tasks." },
        { "id": "r2q17", "question_text": "Virtual meetings fully replicate informal, unplanned workplace conversations.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "False", "explanation": "The passage says virtual meetings struggle to replicate these unplanned exchanges." },
        { "id": "r2q18", "question_text": "There is strong industry-wide agreement on the ideal ratio of office to remote days.", "type": "multiple_choice", "options": ["True", "False", "Not Given"], "correct_answer": "False", "explanation": "The passage says there is no strong consensus on an optimal split." },
